# CI/CD Guide — Phase 2 (Jenkins + SonarQube + Trivy + Docker Hub)

> Predecessor: `POC_GUIDE.md` (Phase 1 — ✅ verified). This file is the **walkthrough** for Phase 2.
>
> **Status (Phase 2 in progress):** Jenkins runs on Oracle Ubuntu at `http://localhost:8080`; **Checkout, SonarScanner check, and SonarQube analysis are working and green** on `main`. This guide now covers finishing the pipeline: **Docker build → Trivy scan (server on an Azure VM) → push to Docker Hub**. Progress checklist: §9.
>
> **This box has no public IP.** Jenkins, SonarQube, and the build host are all the same Oracle Ubuntu machine, reached through `localhost`. GitHub cannot reach in, so the trigger is **polling** (§5.3) — no webhook. The one outbound-but-not-inbound exception is the **Trivy server on an Azure VM**: Jenkins (client) reaches *out* to it over its public address — no inbound path to this box is required.
>
> Requirement source: `requirement` + README §6.1. Stack constraints from the user: **Jenkins** (declarative pipeline), **SonarQube** (quality gate), **Trivy** (image security scan, **client/server — server hosted on an Azure VM**). Container registry is **Docker Hub**, repo **`sachinmaskar/voting`**.

----

## 1. Goals of Phase 2

1. Every commit to `main` triggers a Jenkins pipeline (within ~3 min via **Poll SCM** — §5.3; no webhook on this box).
2. Code is statically analyzed by SonarQube; a failing **quality gate blocks** the build.
3. Images are built, scanned with **Trivy client → server on an Azure VM** (fail on CRITICAL/HIGH), and pushed to **Docker Hub `sachinmaskar/voting`** tagged `<svc>-<sha8>` (immutable) + `<svc>` (rolling).
4. Nothing about Phase 1 behavior changes — same images, same endpoints, now delivered by a pipeline.

**Out of scope (belongs to Phase 3):** Terraform, EKS, Helm, K8s, and the staging/prod deploy stages. The pipeline ends at **push**; deploy is Phase 3.

---

## 2. Pipeline overview (matches the committed `Jenkinsfile`)

```
GitHub commit ──(Poll SCM ~3 min; no webhook §5.2)──▶ Jenkins (declarative Jenkinsfile, repo root)
  Stage 1  Checkout            → git clone github.com/Sachin-dev-hub/voting (branch main)
  Stage 2  Check SonarScanner  → verify the SonarScanner tool is installed (--version)
  Stage 3  SonarQube Analysis  → sonar-scanner → server (projectKey=my-first-project); gate policy
  Stage 4  Docker Build        → docker build backend, frontend, voter-simulator
                                 tagged <svc>-<sha8> + <svc>   (e.g. backend-a1b2c3d4, backend)
  Stage 5  Trivy Scan          → trivy client → server on Azure VM (fail on CRITICAL/HIGH)
  Stage 6  Docker Push         → docker push sachinmaskar/voting:<svc>-<sha8> + :<svc>
                                 (first pushes the immutable tag, then the rolling one)
```

Three services are built and pushed, each mapped to a tag suffix on the single Docker Hub repo:

| Service | Build context | Rolling tag | Immutable tag |
|---|---|---|---|
| backend | `./backend` | `sachinmaskar/voting:backend` | `sachinmaskar/voting:backend-<sha8>` |
| frontend | `./frontend` | `sachinmaskar/voting:frontend` | `sachinmaskar/voting:frontend-<sha8>` |
| voter-simulator | `./voter-simulator` | `sachinmaskar/voting:voter-simulator` | `sachinmaskar/voting:voter-simulator-<sha8>` |

> Docker Hub does **not** support nested repositories (`owner/voting/backend`), so services are distinguished by tag suffix on the single `sachinmaskar/voting` repo. GHCR (used in older draft notes) would allow nesting — irrelevant now.

